---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { crew, subagentKinds } from "../../data/docs-content"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="subagents">

          <p>
            Vector's agent does not have to do every part of a task itself. It can hand self-contained pieces of the
            work to other agents, which run at the same time. There are two kinds, and Vector uses the same two names in
            the chat, in Background tasks, and in Settings → Agents.
          </p>
          <div class="doc-grid doc-grid-4">
            <article class="doc-card">
              <small>General purpose · for big tasks</small>
              <h4>Subagents</h4>
              <p>A subagent takes whatever piece of the task the main agent hands it: answer a question about the code, change a group of files, find out why a check fails. Vector uses them for big tasks, one per independent part, launched together so they run at the same time, and does small tasks itself. You can turn them off in Settings → Agents.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
              <small>Named · one focus each</small>
              <h4>Subagent specialists</h4>
              <p>A subagent specialist is a named agent with a fixed focus and its own permissions. Vector calls one when part of the task fits that focus, such as reviewing a diff or finding the cause of a failure. Agents you define yourself count as custom specialists.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
          <h2 id="how-they-differ">How they differ</h2>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            {subagentKinds.map(([term, detail]) => (<div><dt>{term}</dt><dd>{detail}</dd></div>))}
          </dl>
          <h2 id="specialists">The eight subagent specialists</h2>
          <p>
            Explore, Review, Judge and Security can read your repository and run checks, but they cannot edit it. Debug,
            Test, Performance and Migration can edit. To ask for one yourself, type <code>@</code> and its name in the
            composer.
          </p>
          <div class="doc-grid doc-grid-3">
            {crew.map((member) => (
              <article class="doc-card">
                <small>{member.summary}{member.readOnly ? " · read-only" : ""}</small>
                <h4>{member.name}</h4>
                <p>{member.role}</p>
              </article>
            ))}
          </div>
          <h2 id="where-you-see-them">Where you see them</h2>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>In the chat</strong><span>Each batch of delegated work appears as a card in the conversation, with its title, kind, how many agents are working, their status and the time so far, and one square per agent. Click it to open that work in Background tasks.</span></li>
            <li><strong>In Background tasks</strong><span>Open the panel with the Tasks button in the session header, or with Cmd+Shift+B (Ctrl+Shift+B on Windows and Linux). Each card shows the kind, the elapsed time, how many agents and their tokens, and one row per agent with its model, tokens and time. A running card has a Stop button, running work is shown in purple, and finished tasks collect under Finished.</span></li>
            <li><strong>In Settings</strong><span>Settings → Agents explains both kinds and has the General subagents switch, which turns subagents off. Subagent specialists keep working when it is off.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <h2 id="turning-them-off">Turning subagents off</h2>
          <p>
            Subagents are on by default. With them off, Vector's agent does the work itself, except parts that fit a
            subagent specialist, which it can still hand to one. You can also still ask for a specialist yourself with
            <code>@</code> and its name.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>In the desktop app</strong><span>Switch off General subagents in Settings → Agents. Changing it stops any work in progress, and the new setting applies from your next message.</span></li>
            <li><strong>In the terminal</strong><span>The terminal reads its own config file, so the desktop switch does not change it. From 1.99.99, add <code>{'"agent": { "general": { "disable": true } }'}</code> to <code>~/.config/vector/vector.json</code> (<code>%USERPROFILE%\.config\vector\vector.json</code> on Windows). It takes effect the next time you start Vector in the terminal.</span></li>
            <li><strong>For one repository</strong><span>From 1.99.99, put the same setting in the repository's <code>vector.json</code>. It turns subagents off for that repository in the terminal and the desktop app alike, whatever the switch says. Releases before 1.99.99, such as desktop 1.99.8 and npm CLI 1.99.7, do not read <code>vector.json</code>.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <h2 id="custom-agents">Custom agents</h2>
          <p>
            Explore, review, judge and security carry a read-only boundary that is re-applied after your repository's config is merged, so a checked-in file cannot turn a read-only reviewer into one that writes; they can still run verification commands from an allowlist such as <code>git diff</code>, <code>git log</code> and the repository's test and build scripts.
          </p>
          <p>
            A parent can declare which paths each subagent owns, and Vector refuses two siblings that claim overlapping paths; tasks can depend on each other, run in the background and be resumed later by id.
          </p>
          <p>
            You can add your own:
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector agent create --description "reviews SQL migrations" --mode subagent
vector agent list</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <h3 id="agent-files">Agent files</h3>
          <p>
            An agent is a Markdown file in an <code>agents/</code> or <code>agent/</code> folder: the repository's
            <code>.vector/agents/</code>, <code>~/.vector/agents/</code>, or <code>agents/</code> in Vector's own config
            folder, which <a href="/docs/vector-agent#repository-rules">Repository rules</a> says where to find. The
            file's path is the agent's name, so <code>.vector/agents/sql-review.md</code> is <code>sql-review</code>;
            the frontmatter holds its settings and the body is its prompt. <code>vector agent create</code> writes such
            a file in your config folder or the repository's <code>.vector/agents/</code>. A repository's agents load
            once you trust it.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`---
description: Reviews SQL migrations for locking, data loss and missing rollbacks.
mode: subagent
model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
temperature: 0.1
steps: 30
permission:
  edit: deny
  bash:
    "*": ask
    "git diff*": allow
---
You review SQL migrations. For each one, say which tables it locks and for how long,
whether it can lose data, and whether it can be rolled back.`}</code></pre>
          <h3 id="agent-fields">Agent fields</h3>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>description</dt><dd>When to use the agent. Vector's agent reads it to decide what to hand this one.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>mode</dt><dd><code>primary</code> for an agent you pick to answer, <code>subagent</code> for one the main agent hands work to and you can call with <code>@</code> and its name, or <code>all</code> for both, the default for an agent you add.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>model</dt><dd>The model it runs on, as <code>provider/model</code>.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>variant</dt><dd>The effort level it uses when it runs on its own <code>model</code>.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>prompt</dt><dd>Its instructions. In a file, the body is the prompt.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>temperature and top_p</dt><dd>Sampling settings for its requests. Vector sends a temperature only to a model that takes one.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>steps</dt><dd>How many model steps it may take before it has to answer in text alone.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>permission</dt><dd>Rules of its own, which come after your general rules; see <a href="/docs/permissions#rule-order">Which rule decides</a>.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>color</dt><dd>A hex colour such as <code>#3B82F6</code>, or <code>primary</code>, <code>secondary</code>, <code>accent</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>warning</code>, <code>error</code> or <code>info</code>.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>hidden</dt><dd><code>true</code> keeps a subagent out of the <code>@</code> list; Vector's agent can still hand it work.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>disable</dt><dd><code>true</code> removes the agent. For one of the eight specialists it is the same as switching it off in Settings → Agents.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>any other key</dt><dd>Sent to the model provider as an option with each of the agent's requests.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <h3 id="agents-in-vector-json">Agents in vector.json</h3>
          <p>
            The <code>agent</code> key in <code>vector.json</code> takes the same fields, with the prompt as text or as
            <code>{"{file:./path}"}</code> (see <a href="/docs/configuration#substitution">Configuration files</a>). An
            entry named after a built-in agent changes that agent: <code>build</code> is the main agent and
            <code>plan</code> is Plan mode. The read-only specialists stay read-only, since your rules can only narrow
            them. <code>default_agent</code> names the agent that answers when you do not pick one; it must be a primary
            agent that is not hidden.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "default_agent": "build",
  "agent": {
    "build": { "model": "anthropic/claude-opus-5" },
    "sql-review": {
      "description": "Reviews SQL migrations for locking, data loss and missing rollbacks.",
      "mode": "subagent",
      "prompt": "{file:./prompts/sql-review.md}",
      "permission": { "edit": "deny" }
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
          <p class="note">
            Past six running siblings Vector tells the model to mention the spend, because each subagent spends on your keys.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            Subagents are not agent workspaces. A subagent works inside your session and your checkout and reports to the
            main agent. An agent workspace is a separate session in its own worktree or copy that you start, watch in the
            Agent Dashboard, and merge yourself. See <a href="/docs/parallel-agents">Parallel agents</a>.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
